rustgis-grass-plugin (0.11.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The panel is four steps instead of one column of 24 widgets. RustGIS draws
    a panel as a flat list with no sections, so everything GRASS needs -- the
    database, the layer and region, the terrain runs and the module catalogue,
    four trees among them -- was several screens of scrolling. A Step dropdown
    now chooses one of Project & data, Layer & region, Terrain and Modules, and
    every answer sets each widget's visible from it, so at most eleven of the
    25 widgets are drawn at once. The chosen layer, the region and Last run
    stay visible wherever they are acted on.
  * A step decides what an answer carries as well as what is drawn. The module
    tree is 1,424 nodes and about 270 KB of JSON and a mapset's can be
    thousands of maps, and RustGIS keeps what an answer leaves out, so each
    tree is sent only by the step that draws it. Two new commands answer only
    what changed: layer-pick for a layer choice (five widgets, no catalogue)
    and module-pick for a module choice (three widgets, no host request).
    panel-step shows a step. run-module now takes the module in the command's
    own parameters ({"module": "r.slope.aspect"}), which is how a script runs
    one without bringing the panel to its module step first.
  * Run Module is disabled for a module the panel cannot drive, with the reason
    in Chosen module, rather than failing after the click. Add Map is disabled
    until a map rather than a project or a mapset is chosen.
  * The Catalogue filter, a three-node tree in a 320 px scroll area, is a
    dropdown; its values are now all, imagery and temporal, and the mode/ ids
    of the tree it replaces are still read.
  * The ribbon tab is 122 entries instead of 141, in seven groups: GRASS, Data,
    Region, Terrain, Imagery, Vector and Modules. GRASS's own keywords had put
    the 64 vector tools into 27 menus, 20 of them holding a single tool and two
    of them (LIDAR and Lidar) differing only in case; they are nine themed
    menus now, with the keyword kept as each tool's toolbox for the Tools pane
    and its search. The panel was declared in three groups and is declared
    once, and the three catalogue filters moved into a menu.
  * Every command and menu carries an icon of its own. 32 of 38 commands had
    the same stats icon and the 64 tools and 27 menus had none.
  * minRustgisVersion is 0.6.0, not 0.5.0. The panel's form and choice items
    came after 0.5.0 and a panel item kind an older RustGIS does not know is a
    hard manifest error, so the 0.5.0 floor had the whole plugin refused rather
    than loaded with a widget missing.
  * Two panel picks, which RustGIS 0.6.0 added. Viewpoint on the Map is where
    r.viewshed looks from: it ran from the centre of the map before, whatever
    the user wanted. Without a pick it still falls back to the centre, and the
    run says which it used and where that landed in the raster's own
    projection. Region from a Box draws the computational region on the map,
    the third way GRASS users expect to set it beside the map view and the
    whole layer; its region-box command is also on the ribbon. A box drawn
    across the antimeridian is refused, since the Analysis Environment's
    extent is four increasing degrees.
  * The GRASS workspaces (.gxw) found in the database and under the home
    folder are listed above the projects, and Load Workspace opens one:
    its layers in their ticked state, its coordinate system and its
    extent. It clears the project's layers, so a project with unsaved
    changes stops it unless discard is passed; it cannot replace the
    project itself, which RustGIS reserves to the user.
  * A mapset's imagery groups are listed above its maps, and Add Map
    composes one into a single RGB layer: d.rgb for three bands or more,
    d.his for two, in the group's own band order. A group of one band is
    refused, advising the band be added on its own.
  * Add Map puts the view in the GRASS project's own coordinate system,
    read from g.proj -g, unless the view already holds layers drawn in
    another EPSG system. A map from an XY project is refused with its
    reason named. Add Map also takes a map parameter, for scripts.
  * Every raster command declares a timeout of a day, so the command line,
    Python and scripts no longer cut off r.watershed on a large DEM. The
    desktop app still stops any command at 120 seconds whatever the manifest
    says, and nothing tells the plugin which host it is in, so a run keeps to
    120 seconds unless the caller passes timeout in the command's own
    parameters (1 to 86400 seconds; anything else is refused, not ignored).
    A run that runs out now says how long it was given.
  * The first run says how long it will take and shows how far it has got.
    Generating the manifest reads one GRASS form per module and printed
    nothing for the whole wait; it now counts the forms as it reads them and
    reports to MetaMonitor where mmctl is installed, and
    rustgis-grass-setup, the README and the man page say what the wait is for
    before it starts.
  * A module parameter that names a space-time dataset is a dropdown of the
    chosen mapset's own datasets, read with t.list in a throwaway mapset so
    the user's is never locked, and a parameter naming a 3D raster is one of
    its grid3 maps. 23 more modules run (252 in all): t.rast.list,
    t.rast.colors, t.rast.univar, t.rast.what, t.vect.univar and the rest of
    the 27 that take a dataset. No module is refused for naming a dataset any
    more; one that must name a map it writes still is, and now says so --
    t.rast.to.rast3 reported a missing 3D raster when what it needs is a name
    for the one it writes.
  * A raster module is no longer refused for a required parameter the panel's
    form already offers. That rule was written before the form existed and
    never learned about it, so r.buffer was listed "needs distances" with its
    Distance zone(s) field filled in right above the greyed-out button. 72
    more raster modules run (160 on that path, 348 in all): r.buffer, r.lake,
    r.carve, r.catchment, i.topo.corr, i.modis.qc and the r.li.* family. Only
    a plain value counts, because that path runs in a throwaway project where
    a typed imagery group, vector or dataset name would name nothing, and a
    required field left empty is caught before the export and the GRASS run.
  * A parameter that takes several numbers is a text field, not a number box:
    r.buffer takes distances=100,200,500 and one box holds one zone.
  * A raster run says which parameters the form supplied instead of claiming
    GRASS's own defaults whatever was sent.
  * run-module takes mapset ("<project>/<mapset>") and values (the module's
    parameters by their form field ids) beside module, so a script reaches a
    module in one call instead of walking the panel there in four: a tree
    keeps a value only while it was among the nodes last sent, and a form
    takes only the fields it holds. The parameters are laid over the panel and
    go through the same checks, so a mapset that is not there, a dataset the
    mapset does not hold and a taken output name are refused exactly as from
    the panel.
  * A module that writes a new map or dataset is given a name for it in the
    form, and the name is checked before GRASS starts: its shape, and whether
    the chosen mapset already holds one by that name. A taken name is refused
    by name and --overwrite is never passed, so a name typed in haste costs an
    error message and not a map; a multiple output takes a comma list and a
    single one refuses one. 24 more modules run (276 in all), among them
    t.rast.series, t.rast.aggregate, t.rast.mapcalc, t.rast.extract,
    t.rast.patch and t.rename. A file path to write (10 modules) and a new
    database table (8) stay refused, and the reason now says which of the two
    it is instead of "an output name".
  * A workspace is read from where its project actually is. A .gxw records the
    database it was written for, and these files outlive the machine they were
    made on: one from 2021 naming /home/yann/grassdata and the project
    Normandie failed, because that database exists and its Normandie does not
    -- the project had gone to a backup drive with the workspace file beside
    it. The folder a .gxw is kept in is now tried too, and its name taken as
    the project so a renamed copy opens, then this machine's database; a place
    counts only where the mapset's WIND is. Of 26 workspaces found here, 7
    could not open from their recorded path and 4 now do. One whose project is
    nowhere is still listed, labelled "project not found" with Load Workspace
    disabled, and pressing it names every database tried.
  * The panel no longer tells a module it takes no raster when Run Module runs
    it: a temporal or display module was shown the raster path's reason for a
    path it was never on.
  * The manifest's version is 0.11.0; its 28 panel items, 43 commands, 64
    tools and 7 ribbon groups.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Mon, 16 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.10.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Display modules (d.*) now run from the panel: instead of drawing to a
    monitor they render with GRASS's cairo driver into a picture, which is
    imported back as an RGB raster layer. 19 display modules render this way,
    and the form offers to keep the PNG preview as well. Modules that need a
    live display, edit a map in place, or cannot run in this GRASS build keep
    their reason.
  * The catalogue now marks 88 raster, 116 mapset and 19 display modules as
    runnable (223 in all) out of 1,177 readable modules. The mapset runnable
    count includes 25 modules that change a map of the chosen mapset in place,
    such as r.colors and v.db.update: their map parameters are choices drawn
    from that mapset. The tree, module
    labels and details say when a module writes a map and when it draws a
    picture.
  * The manifest's version is 0.10.0; its 24 panel items, 38 commands, 64
    tools and 7 ribbon groups are unchanged.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Sun, 08 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.9.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Run Module now runs a temporal, database or general module from the
    panel's form in the GRASS project and mapset chosen under Projects and
    mapsets: t.in.era5 imports its space-time dataset there. 91 more modules
    run this way (179 in all); required form fields are checked first, and a
    required raster, dataset or output name the form cannot name keeps its
    reason.
  * A module command may declare "timeout" seconds in plugin.json (1 to
    86400); run-module asks for a day, so a download is not stopped after two
    minutes.
  * The manifest now carries 24 panel items, 38 commands, 64 tools and 7
    ribbon groups, and its version is 0.9.0.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Fri, 06 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.8.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The panel gains a Module parameters form: choosing a module fills it with
    that module's own parameters, read from its GRASS interface description,
    and Run Module sends the values it holds, so a tree-driven module no
    longer runs on GRASS's defaults alone. Fields are text, whole or
    fractional numbers, flags as checkboxes, choices as selection lists, and
    several choices as multi-selection lists; required fields are marked,
    defaults are shown, a range such as i.pca's rescale=0,255 stays a text
    field, and a field left empty is left out so GRASS applies its own
    default. Only the 88 modules that read one raster and write one can run
    from here; the rest still show their parameters but keep their reason.
  * The manifest now carries 24 panel items, 38 commands, 64 tools and 7
    ribbon groups, and its version is 0.8.0.

 -- Yann Chemin <dr.yann.chemin@gmail.com>  Thu, 05 Nov 2026 12:00:00 +0100

rustgis-grass-plugin (0.7.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The panel's catalogue gains a Temporal mode. The mode tree now lists All
    modules, Imagery and Temporal with their counts, and the temporal mode
    shows only the 74 t.* modules.
  * A new Temporal ribbon group, between Vector and Modules, holds the panel
    and a Temporal Modules button that opens that mode. This is a catalogue,
    not a runner: a temporal module reads a space-time dataset rather than one
    raster, so every t.* module keeps its reason in the tree instead of
    offering a run that would fail.
  * The manifest now carries 38 commands, 64 tools, 7 ribbon groups and 23
    panel items, against 1,176 readable modules on this machine. The manifest's
    version is 0.7.0.

